  "content": {
    "id": "p27-q5-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "5",
    "prompt": "The polynomial equation $x^4+ax^3+bx^2+cx+d=0$ has roots $\\alpha,\\beta,\\gamma,\\delta$.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p27-q5-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2=a^2-2b$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2=(-a)^2-2b=a^2-2b$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2&=(\\alpha+\\beta+\\gamma+\\delta)^2-2(\\alpha\\beta+\\alpha\\gamma+\\alpha\\delta+\\beta\\gamma+\\beta\\delta+\\gamma\\delta)\\\\&=(-a)^2-2b=a^2-2b\\end{aligned}$$\nAs required.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p27-q5-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Hence, show that $x^4-3x^3+5x^2+4x-1=0$ cannot have four real roots.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "The sum of the squares of the roots is $(-3)^2-2(5)=-1<0$, which is impossible if all four roots are real.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{aligned}\\alpha^2+\\beta^2+\\gamma^2+\\delta^2&=a^2-2b\\\\&=(-3)^2-2(5)\\\\&=-1<0\\end{aligned}$$\nIf all four roots were real, each square would be non-negative, so their sum would be non-negative. Therefore the equation cannot have four real roots.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 20
      }
    ]
  },
